From: AsherJN
Subject: [PATCH] server: Write the periodic registry save in a child process on macOS.

Adapted for CrossOver 26.3 from AsherJN/recall commit a436e4cd4fac44693b4d699acc030d17eec6f36e
(patches/wine-server-registry-save.patch, without its change log). A
downstream fix, not a WineHQ backport.

Every 30 seconds wineserver writes the dirty registry branches to disk
inside its request loop, so every client request waits for the write.
With a large branch that is a stall of a large fraction of a second for
every process of the session.

On macOS, fork a child that writes the dirty branches and exits with
_exit(), after closing every inherited descriptor so that clients
still see the server go away if it dies first. The parent marks the branches clean when the child starts, so
later changes dirty them again; if the child fails, the branches it was
writing are marked dirty for the next save. A save still running when the
next one is due is left alone, and killed at the first save due
after 60 seconds.
flush_registry() waits for a running child before it writes, so an older
snapshot never replaces a newer one. Where fork() fails the save is
written in the request loop as before. Other platforms keep the old
behaviour, because their SIGCHLD handler reaps children without telling
the registry.

